
The tendency to foresee, and to a lesser extent vocalize, the issues which are likely to arise when testing a new design is a reliable indicator of a wise mind. Wisdom is not a measurement of one's ability to innovate — it is not a result of intelligence. Wisdom is a skill to be honed that allows one to learn from a mistake before it is made. It can be gained through experience and expressed as a possession, but the attribute of "being wise" indicates an ability to borrow one's wisdom from other minds deemed to be trustworthy (or steal it from those deemed otherwise).

The major advantage of wisdom is its paradoxical nature of growing in an amount directly relative to that which it is used. The major shortcoming of wisdom is that anxiety and despair are often an embodiment of its compounding accrual in the absence of its utilization. 

